<div
  class="modal fade"
  id="PAYAL"
  tabindex="-1"
  role="dialog"
  aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel"
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
    <div class="modal-content">
      <div class="modal-header">
        <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">PAYAL JANGID</h5>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">
          X
        </button>
      </div>
      <div class="modal-body">
        <p>
          At 11, Payal Jangid defied child marriage in her village of Heensla,
          Rajasthan. Twice elected Bal Sarpanch under the Bal Mitra Gram
          program, she helped 76 children return to school, stopped six child
          marriages, and fought for dignity and education. Her work has taken
          her to Sweden for the World’s Children’s Prize, earned her the
          Goalkeepers Award (2019) and Reebok’s “Fit to Fight” award, and global
          recognition as a youth leader. Now 21, she is pursuing a B.Ed.,
          preparing for the Rajasthan Administrative Services, and teaching
          village children in her spare time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div
  class="modal fade"
  id="THANGAMMAL"
  tabindex="-1"
  role="dialog"
  aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel"
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
    <div class="modal-content">
      <div class="modal-header">
        <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">THANGAMMAL</h5>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">
          X
        </button>
      </div>
      <div class="modal-body">
        <p>
          For Thangammal, समर्पण is muscle memory. At 80, she still free-dives
          off Pamban Island in Tamil Nadu, sari-clad, holding her breath for
          minutes to harvest seaweed that sustains her family. She follows a
          12-day lunar cycle to protect marine life and rotates across 21
          islands to ease pressure on reefs. Keeper of generational knowledge,
          she mentors younger women in diving, sales, and survival, while
          joining women-led groups to share boats and risks in protected waters.
          With cloth-wrapped fingers, T-shirts over saris, and rubber slippers,
          she makes this hard work a little safer. Her life is a story of समर्पण
          &#8211; to her community, her sisterhood, and the sea.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div
  class="modal fade"
  id="RUBY"
  tabindex="-1"
  role="dialog"
  aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel"
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
    <div class="modal-content">
      <div class="modal-header">
        <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">RUBY PAREEK</h5>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">
          X
        </button>
      </div>
      <div class="modal-body">
        <p>
          At 55, Ruby Pareek from Dausa, Rajasthan, has spent two decades
          transforming farming and lives. After losing her father to cancer, she
          turned to organic methods, determined to stop “feeding poison” to the
          next generation. With NABARD’s support in 2006, Ruby expanded her work
          beyond her own fields, freely sharing earthworms, compost sprays, and
          techniques, especially with women. Her efforts not only improved farms
          but also gave women the courage to step beyond purdah and earn
          livelihoods. From a single vermicomposting unit, her mission has
          reached over 26,000 people, including 11,000 women. Today at her
          Didwana center, she continues to grow more than crops—she nurtures
          dignity, empowerment, and faith in the soil itself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div
  class="modal fade"
  id="APARAJITA"
  tabindex="-1"
  role="dialog"
  aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel"
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
    <div class="modal-content">
      <div class="modal-header">
        <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">APARAJITA DATTA</h5>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">
          X
        </button>
      </div>
      <div class="modal-body">
        <p>
          Since 2002, Aparajita Datta has worked in the forests of Arunachal
          Pradesh, discovering the Arunachal macaque, leading the first wildlife
          census, and helping tribes shift from hunting to education and
          healthcare. Author of 90+ publications and field guides, she has saved
          hornbills, and fought for the endangered leaf deer and black barking
          deer. A wildlife ecologist with the Nature Conservation Foundation,
          she is a Whitley Award winner and Co-Chair of the IUCN Hornbill
          Specialist Group. In every hornbill’s return and every tree restored,
          her koshish lives on. Aparajita lives on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
